// GEORGE BOO'L'S PLATFORM-FIGHTER FACET — his values, in the repository that
// owns character-authored material.
//
//  this file MOVED here from `game/ambition_demo_smash/assets/fighters/` on
// 2026-08-19, on the 2026-08-19 GPT review's finding: *"Smash should select
// George; it should not own George's values."* The demo's own module called it
// "THIS DEMO'S CHARACTER PACKAGE", which was the wrong physical owner for
// exactly the reason this repository's README states — it owns
// "game/ruleset-specific character FACET VALUES", and the Rust repo owns the
// schema, preparation and runtime meaning those values conform to.
//
//  the review's scope fence, kept: ONE honest George package is the acceptance
// criterion.  do NOT migrate the rest of the roster's facets here yet, and
//  do NOT flatten George's sixteen composed moves into RON — representation
// and ownership are separate questions, and "don't flatten this" is settled
// while "game-side Rust is the permanent home" is not.
//
// The consuming pack is `game/ambition_demo_smash/assets/pack.ron`, which now
// SELECTS this file by relative path rather than carrying a copy of it.

// George Boo'l's platform-fighter facet — the excluded middle, as VALUES.
//
//  these numbers were Rust literals in `src/george_booul_moveset.rs` until
// D166's first facet landed. They moved verbatim, with the reasoning that
// justified them, because the reasoning is the character: a value with no reason
// beside it is the thing that gets "balanced" by somebody who does not know what
// it was for.
//
//  the rest of George's sixteen slots stay in Rust on purpose. They are
// authored by COMPOSING helpers (`strike`, `impulse`, `on_hit`,
// `committed_tail`, `feel`) and his file carries a `debug_assert` stating a law
// about the shape of his whole table; flattening that into RON would turn
// authored reasoning into a wall of numbers. The capture kit came out clean.
(
    character: "smash_george_booul",
    // ⭐ HE IS THE HEAVY, and this is where he says so.
    //
    // ⛔ this number was a `match definition.id` arm in the Smash demo's own
    // Rust until 2026-08-31 — a game-owned table writing `Vitals::knockback_
    // weight`, an ordinary character fact the engine already owns, keyed by
    // character id. `character-authoring-package.md` names that shape as a
    // falsifier in as many words, and George is the one fighter this repository
    // authors, so he is the one who stops being described from outside.
    //
    // ⭐ 1.35 IS A SPREAD, NOT AN ABSOLUTE. `scaled_knockback` DIVIDES the growth
    // term by it, so 1.35 means a launch that sends the reference body 100px
    // sends George 74. The stage is tuned against the middleweight at 1.0; his
    // whole design is that he commits and then survives the punish, and a
    // heavy that launches like everyone else has traded away his slow grab and
    // his 0.42 down-throw for nothing.
    knockback_weight: Some(1.35),
    capture: (
        //  the excluded middle, applied to a grab. George's table has three
        // fast options and eight slow ones and nothing in between; his grab
        // belongs with the eight. 0.16 of startup is slower than every grab in
        // the genre and slower than his own jab by more than three times, and the
        // 0.30 of recovery means a whiffed grab is the worst thing he can do —
        // which is the point. A body with no medium answer should not have a safe
        // grab either.
        //
        //  it was authored at 0.14 and HIS OWN LAW refused it: the
        // `debug_assert` at the bottom of `george_booul_moveset()` fired with "a
        // George move landed between the pokes and the commitments", because 0.14
        // sits inside the (0.08, 0.15) gap that IS this character. The number
        // moved to the commitment side rather than the gap moving.
        //
        //  the reach is LONG and SHALLOW (26 forward, 13 tall against his own
        // 24-tall body): he is the heaviest fighter on the grid and he reaches,
        // he does not lunge. The hold sits at 20 forward — out at arm's length,
        // where a logician holds something he is examining.
        grab: (
            id: "george_grab",
            clip: "grab",
            startup_s: 0.16,
            active_s: 0.06,
            recover_s: 0.30,
            reach: (
                offset: (18.0, 0.0),
                half_extents: (26.0, 13.0),
                hold_offset: (20.0, -2.0),
            ),
        ),
        //  a SLOW pummel that hurts. 0.24 is a long beat — most of the genre
        // pummels twice in that time — and 4 is nearly a jab. George trades rate
        // for weight here exactly as he does everywhere else, and the tempo is
        // what makes the trade legible rather than the number.
        pummel: (
            id: "george_pummel",
            clip: "attack",
            duration_s: 0.24,
            impact_at_s: 0.11,
            impact: (damage: 4),
        ),
        //  the release is LATE in the move (0.20 of a 0.34 timeline), which
        // is what makes the wind-up readable and the throw punishable if it is
        // predicted. The launch is nearly flat (-0.35 of vertical against 1.0
        // forward): George throws ACROSS the stage rather than up, so his throw
        // is a stage-control tool and not a combo starter — the heavy body's
        // version.
        forward_throw: (
            id: "george_fthrow",
            clip: "attack",
            duration_s: 0.34,
            release_at_s: 0.20,
            launch: (
                damage: 11,
                knockback: 138.0,
                knockback_growth: 1.9,
                launch_dir: (1.0, -0.35),
            ),
        ),
        //  HIS OTHER THREE THROWS ARE THREE DIFFERENT LOGICAL OPERATIONS,
        // authored 2026-08-20 at Jon's ask. He was the last fighter on the
        // roster still holding one throw, and the three that arrived are NOT his
        // forward throw scaled — that is what the rest of the roster has, where
        // every fighter's back/up/down is its own f-throw times 1.08 / 1.04 /
        // 0.74 and the three answer the same question at three volumes.
        //
        //  the excluded middle governs the RELEASE too. His own law says a
        // move is a poke or a commitment and the band 0.08..0.15 is empty; a
        // throw has no Active window, so `no_move_lives_between_the_pokes_and_
        // the_commitments` cannot see one — which is exactly why these obey it by
        // hand. 0.05, 0.17, 0.28, and his existing 0.20. Nothing in the band.

        // MODUS TOLLENS — deny the consequent and the antecedent goes with
        // it. His ONLY fast capture option, and the cheapest thing in his kit:
        // he does not turn around to throw backward, he simply stops holding and
        // the body is behind him.  the flat 1.3 growth and the near-horizontal
        // launch are the point — this NEVER kills at any percent. What it buys is
        // the side of the stage he wants, which for the heaviest body on the grid
        // is worth more than the damage he gave up to get it.
        back_throw: Some((
            id: "george_bthrow",
            clip: "attack",
            duration_s: 0.15,
            release_at_s: 0.05,
            launch: (
                damage: 5,
                knockback: 104.0,
                knockback_growth: 1.3,
                launch_dir: (-1.0, -0.12),
            ),
        )),
        // THE TAUTOLOGY — P ∨ ¬P, which is true no matter what you put in it.
        //
        //  `knockback_growth: 0.0`, and it is the whole move. `scaled_
        // knockback` short-circuits a zero growth and returns the base untouched,
        // so this throw ignores the victim's percent AND their weight: it sends a
        // fresh featherweight and a 200% heavyweight to the SAME height, every
        // time. Nothing else in the game does that. It is never a kill and never
        // gets worse — a juggle he can rely on the way he relies on a theorem.
        //
        //  145 base is deliberately ABOVE his f-throw's 138, and that is the
        // trade rather than a mistake: it out-launches everything he has at 0%
        // and is the weakest thing he has at 120%, where the f-throw's 1.9 growth
        // has more than doubled it.
        up_throw: Some((
            id: "george_uthrow",
            clip: "attack",
            duration_s: 0.28,
            release_at_s: 0.17,
            launch: (
                damage: 6,
                knockback: 145.0,
                knockback_growth: 0.0,
                launch_dir: (0.0, -1.0),
            ),
        )),
        // REDUCTIO AD ABSURDUM — hold the assumption until it breaks.
        //
        // The slowest move in his capture kit and the hardest-hitting throw in
        // the game: 0.42 of timeline with the release at 0.28, which is longer
        // than his grab's entire startup-to-recovery. He does not put the body
        // down, he drives it into the stage until the position is untenable.
        //
        //  the one throw of his that KILLS, by growth rather than by base:
        // 68 is feeble at 0% and the 2.6 growth is the steepest number in his
        // file. A throw that reads as slow and harmless early and takes a stock
        // at 130% is the same bargain every one of his commitments makes.
        down_throw: Some((
            id: "george_dthrow",
            clip: "attack",
            duration_s: 0.42,
            release_at_s: 0.28,
            launch: (
                damage: 14,
                knockback: 68.0,
                knockback_growth: 2.6,
                launch_dir: (0.22, -0.95),
            ),
        )),
    ),
)
